INT. TRANSCONTINENTAL EXPRESS - DINING CAR - NIGHT

White tablecloths. Silver on linen. Brass lamps with fringed shades sway to the rhythm of the rails. Outside, black prairie slips by.

HELENA MARCHBANKS (40s, evening gown, diamonds at the throat, gloves she has not removed) sits alone at a table set for two. A glass of champagne sweats before her.

CONRAD ELLIS-WHYTE (50s, tuxedo, white carnation, a face of polished walnut) enters from the vestibule. He pauses. Tugs his cuffs. Crosses.

FITZGERALD "FITZ" OLUWASEUN (20s, porter's uniform, tray balanced on one palm) sets down a plate of oysters, glances at both of them, and withdraws toward the pantry door, listening.

                    CONRAD
          You're early.

                    HELENA
          You're late.

                    CONRAD
          I was detained.

                    HELENA
          By whom?

He takes the chair across from her. Smooths the napkin over his knee.

                    CONRAD
          By nobody. By the bar. By myself.

                    HELENA
          That's three.

                    CONRAD
          That's honest.

She lifts her glass. Does not drink. Sets it down.

                    HELENA
          I got your telegram.

                    CONRAD
          Ah.

                    HELENA
          It said "Meet me in the dining car, we need to talk."

                    CONRAD
          It did.

                    HELENA
          It did not say about what.

                    CONRAD
          I thought you'd know.

                    HELENA
          I suspected.

Fitz shifts by the pantry door. A tray rattles. Both look at him. He smiles brightly, bows, vanishes.

                    CONRAD
          Is that boy listening?

                    HELENA
          Everyone listens on a train. It's a very small town.

Conrad leans in. Lowers his voice.

                    CONRAD
          The Havilland papers. You gave them to the Senator.

                    HELENA
          I did.

                    CONRAD
          Without telling me.

                    HELENA
          I told you in the telegram I sent first. You never answered.

                    CONRAD
          I assumed it was a joke.

                    HELENA
          I assumed you read your mail.

He slaps the table. Silverware leaps. The oysters shiver in their ice. Helena doesn't blink.

                    CONRAD
          Do you realize what you have done?

                    HELENA
          I have kept a promise, Conrad. It's unfamiliar to you.

                    CONRAD
          Forty years. Forty years of this firm, and you hand it to a politician over a cup of coffee.

                    HELENA
          Over a lunch.

                    CONRAD
          Oh, a lunch! That makes it civil.

                    HELENA
          It was a good lunch.

He rises. She does not. The car lurches around a curve; a glass slides, drops, shatters. Fitz appears with a brush and a dustpan, sweeps without a word, and retreats.

Conrad sits down again, heavily. Reaches for her hand. She lets him.

                    CONRAD (CONT'D)
          You were always the smarter one.

                    HELENA
          I know.

                    CONRAD
          I resent it.

                    HELENA
          I know that too.

                    CONRAD
          What now?

                    HELENA
          Eat your oysters. They're getting warm.

He laughs, once, a surprised bark. Picks up a fork. The train howls into the dark.
